WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.780
Elon Musk is known to have beef with a lot
of people.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.509
He says he’s not the ‘biggest fan’ of
Warren Buffett

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:07.140
He’s blasted Mark Zuckerberg’s Meta

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.059
And he frequently pokes fun at Jeff Bezos.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:11.709
Jeff who?

00:00:11.709 --> 00:00:20.330
But one guy he really, really, really doesn’t
like is Martin Eberhard, one of Tesla’s

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.980
founders.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.900
The two have squabbled over who actually founded
Tesla.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.910
I’m not sure why he has to say he was a
founder when he wasn’t, I don’t understand

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.079
that.

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:34.430
Yah, whatever.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.860
And he counts himself as one of them.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.260
Martin Eberhard and fellow engineer Mark Tarpenning
incorporated Tesla Motors in 2003.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.559
Ian Wright, JB Straubel, and Elon Musk joined
later.

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:50.680
However, Musk believes there’s no way Tesla
would have even gone into production without

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.280
him and JB Straubel, its Chief Technology
Officer.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.700
Musk pushed Eberhard out of his role as CEO
and eventually, out of the company in 2007.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.790
By then, Musk had been with Tesla for three
years.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:06.869
He used his earnings from PayPal to sign on
as one of the first investors in Tesla in

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.369
2004 and became chairman of the board.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:12.799
Why does Elon Musk despise Martin Eberhard?

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:16.509
He even went so far as to say: “I wish I
had never met him.”

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:21.219
He believes Eberhard nearly drove the company
into the ground when trying to produce Tesla’s

00:01:21.219 --> 00:01:22.850
first car, the Roadster.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.959
You can have a car that’s quick and you
can have a car that’s electric.

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:28.009
But having one that’s both is how you can
make electric cars popular.

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:32.289
Trouble is, Musk didn’t think Eberhard was
capable of building an electric sports car.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:36.709
He says Eberhard wanted to outsource car battery
production to a BBQ manufacturing factory

00:01:36.709 --> 00:01:38.060
in Thailand!

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.959
As they were having trouble finding a factory
willing to produce an advanced lithium-ion

00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:42.959
battery pack.

00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:46.960
When a Tesla team went over to check out the
factory, “Instead of a factory, they found

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.740
a concrete slab with posts holding up a roof.”

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.069
as Ashlee Vance detailed in a book about Elon
Musk.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:57.709
Since the Thai company couldn’t make a battery
pack, it caused issues for the rest of the

00:01:57.709 --> 00:01:58.789
supply chain.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:02.649
The plan was to have the battery cells made
in Japan, shipped to Thailand, where they

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:06.880
were turned into a battery pack, then shipped
to England where a company called Lotus Engineering

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.711
would build the body of the car and attach
the battery pack, before making its way to

00:02:10.711 --> 00:02:13.200
the initial market in California.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.510
Musk alleges Eberhard screwed up in other
areas, too.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.080
He says he outsourced the carbon fiber body
panels to a company that couldn’t make them.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.510
Carbon fiber is a special material.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.250
It’s very strong for its very light weight.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.610
He said the gaps between the body panels were
so big you could put your thumb in between

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.610
them.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.250
Unfortunately, I don’t have photos of the
original roadster’s body panel issues but

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.810
here are photos someone posted on Twitter
of a Model 3 with large gaps between the panels.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.880
Possibly the GREATEST mistake Tesla made in
the early days was deciding on the transmission

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.910
system for the Roadster.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.300
Musk says Eberhard refused to change the motor
size.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.190
So, to achieve zero to 60 (mph) as quickly
as possible WITHOUT a bigger motor, they required

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.410
a two-speed transmission rather than a single-speed
one.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:00.630
The first gear would take the car from zero
to 60 in less than four seconds, the second

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.660
gear would take the car up to 130 mph (210
km/h).

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.210
However, the transmission couldn’t handle
the jump from the first gear to the second.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:13.060
Eberhard had vowed to begin shipping the Roadster
in late 2007 - but there was no way that could

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.920
happen with the transmission issues.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.100
Actually, the original plan was to deliver
the car in 2006.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.810
In the end, production for the Roadster didn’t
begin until the fall of 2008.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.530
Eberhard has responded to Musk’s criticisms.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.580
In a message to me, he wrote: “That is of
course ridiculous.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.020
Sure, we were over-budget and behind schedule.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.490
(Which successful startup companies were not?).

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:40.370
He went on to say: Since I left Tesla 14 years
ago, which of Tesla's cars has been on schedule

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:41.610
or on budget?

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.520
How is that Cybertruck going?

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.280
In reference to Tesla’s futuristic truck.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.910
Musk had said it would make it to market by
the end of 2021 but has now been delayed until

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:51.910
2023.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:57.890
Musk also accuses Eberhard of intentionally
deceiving him about the true cost of the Roadster.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.959
When one of Tesla’s investors sent its managing
director to do an audit, they found that the

00:04:01.959 --> 00:04:07.400
cost of producing the sports car would be
$120,000 - well above the selling price of

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.400
$92,000.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.020
Eberhard had said it would cost $65,000 to
make.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.830
Tesla had already taken deposits from hundreds
of customers, promising the car would cost

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.430
less than $100,000.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.430
Enough was enough and as the Roadster was
way

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.650
over budget and late, Musk got on the phone
with Eberhard, and told him the board had

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:30.550
decided he’d be replaced as CEO, demoting
him to the role of President of Technology

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:35.199
in mid-2007 while keeping the same $200,000
salary he made as CEO.

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:36.610
Eberhard was pissed off.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.750
And by the year’s end, he had left the company
altogether.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:43.389
I was voted off the island in a rather rude
way.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:48.300
Tesla named Michael Marks as interim CEO,
followed by permanent CEO Ze’ev Drori - a

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.800
Silicon Valley semiconductor veteran - before
Musk took over the top job in the fall of

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:53.949
2008.

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:56.310
Musk may have blamed Eberhard for Tesla’s
troubles.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.069
However, Eberhard has blamed Musk for the
Roadster’s problems.

00:05:00.069 --> 00:05:04.610
In this Newsweek article from 2008, Eberhard
accused Musk of interfering with the design

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.349
of the car, demanding changes that were costly
and led to delays.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:12.290
These included installing electronic door
latches, building a lightweight carbon-fiber

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:15.740
body, and lowering the door sill by two inches.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.949
Musk denied that those changes were the cause
of the delays and told Fortune Magazine he

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:25.180
was “too busy trying to fix the (bleeping)
mess he (as in Eberhard) left”

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.639
In 2009, Eberhard sued Musk, alleging Musk
“began a pattern of defaming and disparaging

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:31.659
him” in the media.

00:05:31.659 --> 00:05:36.169
The lawsuit also states Musk “set out to
rewrite history by falsely claiming that he

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.199
was the founder or creator of Tesla Motors.”

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:43.259
When someone on Twitter pointed out Musk was
NOT the founder, Musk argued founding means

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:45.710
more than filing papers to start a company.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:52.229
He says without him, Tesla Motors had “no
employees, no IP address, no designs, no prototype,

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:54.740
literally nothing but a business plan”.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.360
In its counterclaim against Eberhard, Tesla
and Musk dismissed Eberhard’s lawsuit as

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.219
“nothing more than to curb open discourse
on matters of importance to the public and

00:06:04.219 --> 00:06:06.419
to extract money from Tesla….”

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:08.219
In the end, they agreed to a settlement.

00:06:08.219 --> 00:06:13.219
Elon Musk, JB Straubel, and Ian Wright were
allowed to call themselves co-founders alongside

00:06:13.219 --> 00:06:14.839
Mark Tarpenning and Martin Eberhard.

00:06:14.839 --> 00:06:18.870
Oh, and by the way, Eberhard mentioned in
his lawsuit that he didn’t receive the second

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:23.120
Roadster Tesla ever produced as promised,
which Musk sold to a friend.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.830
The one he did finally get had been smashed
into the back of a truck by an employee during

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.279
an endurance test, “almost completely totaling
the vehicle”.

00:06:31.279 --> 00:06:32.900
It was a wreck.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.059
Perhaps a metaphor for the relationship between
Musk and Eberhard.

00:06:37.059 --> 00:06:41.900
After leaving Tesla, Eberhard briefly led
Volkswagen’s EV development in the U.S.,

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:47.470
was the VP of Atieva which is now Lucid Motors
for only six weeks, and also ran his own electric

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:48.470
start-up.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.999
Now, he’s enjoying retirement.

00:06:49.999 --> 00:06:52.349
Well, enjoying it as much as he can.

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:53.689
Except when Musk brings up his name.

00:06:53.689 --> 00:06:55.770
For someone to be the worst person that I’ve
ever worked with, by far, that’s not easy.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.060
Eberhard ended his message to me by saying:

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:02.569
“I find it strange that Musk feels he must
still spend his energy criticizing me, when

00:07:02.569 --> 00:07:07.900
my startup company, with all its flaws, has
revolutionized the entire automotive industry

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.819
and has made Musk the richest man in the world.”

